---
scope: src/tools/init/writeMethodology
intent: >
  writeMethodology installs the host-side pointer stub — the short, marker-
  bounded region inside the host framework's config file (CLAUDE.md,
  .cursorrules, etc.) whose only job is to tell the agent that AIDE exists,
  name the path to the progressively-disclosed `.aide/` doc hub on disk,
  and instruct the agent to crawl that hub before writing or acting on any
  `.aide` file. The stub is deliberately tiny so non-AIDE sessions in the
  host project pay almost nothing to carry it, and the methodology body the
  agent actually needs lives next to the work on disk where it can be read
  on demand. Success is a host project whose config file gains a handful of
  lines of pointer plus a crawl trigger, and loses the several-hundred-line
  methodology dump that used to ship here.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The installed region is a short pointer stub — a handful of lines
      rather than a full methodology body. It names the host-side doc-hub
      path, teaches the agent when to crawl (before writing or acting on
      any `.aide` file), and tells the agent never to guess AIDE rules from
      memory. It contains no canonical AIDE doctrine — the doctrine lives
      in the installed hub, not in the stub.
    - The stub's instructional text is sourced from canonical content read
      through initContent at call time, not from literals baked into this
      helper's source. Framework plumbing (marker comments, headers, the
      rendered host-side path) is composed here; the teaching sentences
      are not.
    - The stub names the host-side doc-hub path explicitly so the agent has
      zero guessing to do about where to look. The path and the location
      the installer helper writes to must always agree; this stub is the
      contract the installer fulfills.
    - Idempotency is still detected via a marker comment. A re-run on a
      host project whose config file already carries the marker returns
      `exists` and writes nothing. Silent upgrades — overwriting a stale
      stub with a newer one in place — are explicitly out of scope and
      belong to a future explicit update path.
    - Content outside the marker-bounded region is preserved verbatim on
      every run. The helper owns only the bytes between its markers; the
      user's existing config content, their team's private conventions,
      and any other agent-facing instructions survive untouched.
  undesired:
    - The stub ships the full concatenated methodology body — the exact
      regression this spec exists to reverse. Every line of canonical
      doctrine inside the config file is a line the agent re-reads in
      every session, AIDE-related or not, and a line that drifts the
      moment the installed hub diverges from the stub.
    - Any AIDE teaching sentence living as a template literal in this
      helper's source. If the stub text needs to say "before writing or
      acting on any `.aide` file, crawl the hub", that sentence must come
      from canonical content read at runtime — the same invariant the
      parent spec locks in for the full-body case still applies to the
      stub.
    - The stub naming the doc-hub path in terms that disagree with where
      the installer helper actually writes the hub. A stub that points at
      `.aide/` while the installer writes into `aide/` is worse than no
      stub at all, because it sends agents to an empty directory and
      teaches them the tooling is unreliable.
    - A re-run that rewrites the stub in place to pick up a doctrine
      edit, silently replacing a user's marker-bounded region without
      them asking. Idempotency here means "do nothing on re-run"; upgrades
      are always explicit.
    - A failure path that falls back to an inline default stub when the
      canonical source is missing or unreadable. Silent fallbacks to
      embedded content are the failure mode the renderer architecture
      exists to prevent, and the stub is no exception.
    - Rewriting the config file from scratch and losing content the user
      had outside the marker-bounded region. The helper is a surgical
      insert, never a replace.
---

## Context

The host project's config file is the primary documentation surface the
agent reads on every session — AIDE-related or not. Every line that lives
between its opening and closing markers is a line every session pays for,
because the agent re-reads the file from the top each time it starts work.
Shipping the full AIDE methodology into that region made the agent pay the
entire doctrine cost on every unrelated task: a bug fix in an unrelated
module, a commit message, a README edit. The cost was invisible per-session
but compounded across every minute of agent time the host project used.

The fix is to treat the config file the same way the rest of AIDE treats
code: the short, high-traffic surface teaches *what exists and where to
look*, and the long, low-traffic surface holds the actual content. The
full methodology already lives in canonical form under this repo's
`docs/`; a sibling helper installs it into the host project as a
progressively-disclosed `.aide/` hub. This helper's job shrinks to
installing the pointer that names that hub and the instruction that
triggers a crawl into it.

The parent spec's renderer invariants still govern — doctrine lives in
`docs/`, this helper never embeds canonical text as literals, marker-based
idempotency is the only supported shape, and content outside the region
is sacred. This spec narrows those invariants to the stub case, not away
from them.

## Strategy

**The delivery layer practices the same progressive disclosure it
teaches.** The methodology's own rule is that agents crawl from a short
pointer into deeper material on demand. Shipping a full-body methodology
into the host config file violated that rule at the delivery layer: the
doctrine that tells agents to crawl on demand was itself dumped up front.
The stub shape restores the invariant — the config file gains a pointer,
the agent crawls into the installed hub only when the task touches AIDE,
and non-AIDE sessions skim past a handful of lines instead of several
hundred.

**Stub text comes from canonical content, framework plumbing does not.**
The sentences the stub uses to teach "AIDE exists, here is the hub, crawl
before acting" are AIDE doctrine and therefore ineligible to live as
string literals in this helper's source. They are read through
initContent from whatever canonical surface the architect chooses — a
dedicated pointer doc, a short section of an existing canonical doc, or
a new canonical doc created for this purpose — and composed into the
stub at call time. The marker comment, the host-side path fragment, and
the framework-specific header around the block are pure plumbing and can
be composed locally. The dividing line is content versus container: if a
sentence teaches an AIDE rule, it comes from `docs/`; if it glues
canonical content into the host's config file shape, it is plumbing.

**Path agreement with the installer is load-bearing.** The stub names the
host-side hub path and the installer writes into that path. They must
agree by construction, not by coincidence — a shared constant, a shared
helper, a shared configuration surface, whatever the architect chooses.
The worst failure mode of this refactor is a stub that points at a path
the installer never created; the agent reads the stub, follows the
pointer, finds nothing, and concludes the tooling is broken. The
architect's plan owns the mechanism; this spec owns the invariant that
the two sides cannot drift.

**Idempotency stays marker-based, upgrades stay explicit.** The marker
check is unchanged: if the opening marker is present in the host config
file, the helper returns `exists` and writes nothing. A re-run does not
detect a stale stub and replace it in place, because a user who has
edited the marker-bounded region for their team's workflow would lose
that edit silently on the next init. Intentional re-sync belongs to a
future explicit update path, not to this helper's happy path. The stub
being short does not change this — if anything, it makes the argument
stronger, because a short stub is easy for a user to customize and
exactly the kind of content a silent upgrade would steamroll.

**Fail loudly when the canonical source is unreachable.** If the
canonical content that feeds the stub cannot be read, the helper returns
an error naming the missing source rather than falling back to an inline
default. The reasoning from the parent still applies: a visible bug
report is strictly better than an invisible regression where the stub
ships stale pointer text that disagrees with the installed hub.

**The surgical-insert invariant survives shrinking.** The region the
helper owns got smaller, but the rule that everything outside it is
sacred got no weaker. The helper finds the insertion point, writes its
marker-bounded region, and leaves every other byte of the config file
alone. A user with their own CLAUDE.md content above or below the
region sees that content untouched across every init.

## Good examples

A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time in a fresh Claude
Code project whose `CLAUDE.md` is empty. The helper writes a short
marker-bounded region at the top of the file — an opening marker
comment, a header naming the methodology and the host-side doc-hub
path, a handful of lines that say AIDE exists, that the full doc set
lives at the hub path, that the agent should crawl the hub before
writing or acting on any `.aide` file, and that the agent should never
guess AIDE rules from memory. A closing marker comment closes the
region. The file is a handful of lines long instead of several
hundred. Every word inside the region that teaches something traces
back to canonical content read through initContent; the only locally-
composed pieces are the marker, the header, and the rendered host-side
path.

A developer runs `aide_init` a second time on the same project. The
helper sees the opening marker, returns `exists`, writes nothing, and
the init summary reports the methodology step as already-present. The
developer also runs init a third time, after manually customizing the
stub's header with their team's name. The customization survives; the
marker is still there; the helper reports `exists`; their edit is
untouched.

A host project's config file has a hundred lines of unrelated agent
instructions already in it when init runs for the first time. The
helper writes its marker-bounded region at the correct insertion
point and leaves every one of those hundred lines byte-identical
above and below. The user's content is sacred outside the region; the
helper is a surgical insert.

An agent session opens the host project on an unrelated task — say,
fixing a bug in a service module. The agent reads `CLAUDE.md`, sees
the short stub, learns that AIDE exists and where the hub is, and
consciously chooses not to crawl the hub because the task does not
touch `.aide` files. Total session cost for carrying the AIDE
methodology was a handful of lines, not several hundred. An hour
later, the same agent picks up a different task that does touch
`.aide` files; this time it follows the stub's pointer, reads the
hub, and crawls into whichever canonical doc its task needs. The
stub and the hub work together exactly the way `study-playbook` and
the user's global brain already do.

## Bad examples

A helper rewritten to look like the stub shape but with the
teaching sentences typed inline as template literals: `const STUB =
"...AIDE is..."`. The canonical doc now lives in two places —
`docs/` and this helper's source — and a later edit to the canonical
source will drift away from the shipped stub. The rewrite technically
solved the "long block" problem and silently created the "two
sources of truth" problem the parent spec exists to prevent.

A stub that points at `aide/` while a sibling helper installs the
hub into `.aide/`. The agent reads the stub, follows the pointer,
finds nothing, and either gives up or invents a different location.
The two sides were never tied together by a shared path source, and
the refactor shipped with the mismatch invisible until an agent hit
it in production.

A re-run that detects an older stub in place, composes a fresh one
from the current canonical source, and writes the new version on top
of the old. The user's hand-edit to the stub header is gone without
a prompt or a warning; the next time they read their CLAUDE.md they
find their customization missing and mistrust the tool. The stub is
small enough to edit by hand, which is exactly why silent upgrades
are worse here than they would have been for a long block.

A helper whose canonical source is missing and which responds by
writing an inline default stub with text typed from memory. The
developer runs init and sees `created`, never knowing that the
region in their config now points at the wrong path or teaches a
stale crawl trigger. The failure mode the renderer architecture
exists to prevent has moved from the full-body case into the stub
case.

A stub installed above a fifty-line block of the user's unrelated
instructions — and a future refactor that "cleans up the insertion
point" by rewriting the whole config file from scratch, wiping the
fifty lines out. The region got smaller; the surgical-insert
invariant got forgotten; the user trusted the tool and lost work.
